{
  "version": 351,
  "released": "2026-09-25",
  "changelog": "v351: the documentation says what the system is at v350. Docs only — no executable diff, no behaviour change, and no CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION move (it stays v350's `timeline-rules:17`). WHY: fifty releases of timeline work (v300-v350) each landed their own changelog paragraph, ADR amendment and handbook row, and nothing ever rewrote the front door — `README.md` still described the placement loop as it stood at v314, named six Interactions where nine are registered, said \"~40 subcommands\" of a CLI with 134, and never named the substrate a reader has to know to follow one answer from typed text to a dated moment. WHAT A READER GETS NOW. `README.md` leads with a one-paragraph mental model (immutable sources, derived everything, one fold, one drawing), then names THE SUBSTRATE once and uses it consistently — source, telling, claim and its seven `claim_type`s, receipt, node, episode, binding envelope, alias, landmark entry and its domain ladder, work item/card and its ten kinds, frame, era, axis membership, the spine, the resolver, the fold — each with the artifact it writes; then HOW A MEMORY BECOMES A DATED MOMENT, one answer walked end to end in seven steps, each naming what it writes (source -> classification -> receipt -> binding envelope -> projection + work items -> resolution ledger); then HOW THE TIMELINE IS COMPUTED — the nine placement bases with `chronology.BASIS_WEIGHT`'s actual weights, containment inside a stay, age arithmetic and v346's `BIRTH_ANCHOR_TIERS`, v336's recency rungs as a STATED placement with both of their refusals, the binder's R2a-R2d with the once-per-subject and once-per-couple kinds, the five laws that protect the drawing (`A_MERGE_NEVER_MOVES_A_DATED_MOMENT` v340, `AN_ALIAS_NEVER_NAMES_A_NODE_THE_DRAWING_PUBLISHES` v342, `A_STATED_ENTRY_IS_NEVER_RETIRED_BY_SHAPE` v349, `NOTHING_TO_BIND_COSTS_NOTHING` v348, and the axis rule), and the five ways a node id moves with what each one reports; then THE OWNER'S RULINGS AS RULES rather than as anecdotes — a card is asked only when a person could answer it (v343/v350), whose moments ride the owner's axis (`timeline-rules:10`, v334/v338), a date card only when narrowing changes something (v336), a dated birthday is a birth and an introduction names one person in one clause (v346/v347), an age and a date corroborate (v345), a landmark is drawn as what it is (v345), the `birth` landmark is the owner's own (v339/v341); then CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION explained as the fold's CONTRACT version rather than a release number; then THE VERBS, every one checked against `system/lifehug.py --help` with what it writes and whether it is dry-run by default. THE RULE-VERSION TABLE lands in `docs/handbook/eras.md` under a new \"The fold's contract version\" heading: every slot from `:8` (v314) to `:17` (v350), with the release that took it and what the same claims now calculate to, derived from this file's own changelog history and from `temporal_timeline`'s notes beside the constant — plus the releases in that range that deliberately did NOT move it and why (v336 work items and display only; v339-v341 the drawing heals on redraw; v348 proved by a byte-identical publish; v349 measured byte-identical). The page keeps its existing prose narrative and its parity annotation, which still pins `timeline-rules:17`. STALE THINGS FOUND AND FIXED. A paragraph about Add Landmark and house nicknames had been pasted under the \"Two users, one Loop\" heading of `README.md` and under \"Nomenclature\" in `README.template.md`, where neither heading had anything to do with it — moved into the Landmarks vocabulary where it belongs. \"Six Interactions\" and \"arc walking is proposed, not built\" (two seats in the README, one in the template): nine are registered in `interactions/registry.json`, and arc_walk has shipped since v193 with its own `interaction.yaml`, module and eval harness. \"~40 subcommands\": 134. `docs/handbook/index.md` listed four Interaction pages and omitted the five that exist. ADR 0037's `Shipped:` header stopped at v326 although the file carries amendments through v350, and its v346 amendment was headed `(v344` — the number its branch carried before v344 and v345 were taken by other releases — now headed v346 with the renumbering stated, matching `temporal_timeline`'s own `timeline-rules:15` (v346) note. THE VERB NAMES, checked rather than trusted. The CLI verb is `resolve`; `resolver` is the module, and several changelog paragraphs spell the command the other way — the README and the handbook now say which is which. `publish` is not a `lifehug.py` subcommand at all: it is `system/temporal_publication.py`'s own entrypoint (`--rebuild`, `--check`) and the function every write path calls, which the verbs table now states. And three resolver flags the docs spelled as `resolve --eval` / `resolve --estimate-missing` / `resolver --bind-restatements` exist only on `system/resolver.py`'s OWN argparse and are not forwarded by the `lifehug.py resolve` wrapper, so the four references across ADR 0037 and the timeline handbook now name the entrypoint that actually has them. The wrapper's flag set is unchanged: this release documents the behaviour rather than altering it. ONE MORE COUNT CORRECTED FROM THE CODE RATHER THAN FROM THE PROSE: v348's own changelog summarises the binder's fast-path signature as \"three content digests plus two version numbers\" and then says its guards prove \"every one of the six signature fields\" gates the match. The code is the six: `episode_binder.BINDER_RECEIPT_SIGNATURE_FIELDS` is `telling_digest`, `bindings_digest`, `landmark_digest`, `rule_version`, `calculation_rule_version` and `framework_version`, checked against a receipt of the matching `BINDER_RECEIPT_SCHEMA_VERSION`. The README and the handbook name all six; `framework_version` is the one the summary sentence dropped. `docs/handbook/timeline.md` gains the substrate nouns beside its existing chronology nouns, the five laws and the five re-key causes as tables naming each constant and its seat, eleven new \"Where it lives\" rows for the modules the last fifty releases added (`temporal_timeline`, `temporal_projection`, `temporal_work_items`, `axis_membership`, `episode_binder`, `episode_fold` and the two contract modules, `identity_resolution`, `roster_relations`, `landmark_projection`, `landmarks_interaction`, `resolver`), and ADR 0037, the I0 fold contract and the eras rule-version table in its Decisions section. `README.template.md` — the personalization starting point a new install copies, which is NOT generated from `README.md` by any make target or test, and which `update.py` protects from overwrite — gains its own short timeline vocabulary and command block, so a personalized vault's front page stops being silent about the half of the product the last two months built. EVERY PATH IN THE NEW SUBSTRATE TABLE WAS RESOLVED BACK TO THE CONSTANT THAT DEFINES IT rather than written from memory, which caught three of this release's own first drafts: the identity store is `state/temporal_claims/identities/{bindings,operations}` (`event_identity.IDENTITY_STATE_DIR`) and not `state/event_identity/`, a human binding is a SOURCE at `sources/identity/{bindings,operations}` (Law 7 — human decisions are sources, not state), and an era's identity, label and kind are sources too, at `sources/eras/` with `memberships/` and `display/` beside them (`lifehug_core.ERA_SOURCES_DIR`), not `state/eras/`. The receipts (`temporal_claims.RECEIPTS_DIR`), the projection and work items (`temporal_projection.PROJECTION_FILE` / `WORK_ITEMS_FILE`), the binder receipt, `state/landmarks.json`, `sources/landmarks/entry-*.md`, `state/resolver/resolutions.json`, `state/classifications/` and `state/source_manifest.json` all check out as written. VERIFIED: full local suite 6686 tests, 6681 passed, 1 failed, 4 skipped, and the checkout clean afterwards. The one failure is the known macOS-only `test_file_answer_outer_queue_leaves_no_plaintext_temp`, and it was re-run alone on a pristine `origin/main` worktree and failed identically: the subprocess it spawns resolves `python3` to this Mac's CommandLineTools 3.9 shim, which dies with `posix_spawn: … Undefined error: 0` before any Lifehug code runs. Nothing in this release is Python, so nothing in it could reach that test. `tests/test_handbook_parity.py` green, including the eras page's `CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION = timeline-rules:17` annotation and every `embed:` block. `tests/test_queue_convergence.py`'s two README assertions green. Every `framework_files` entry still exists on disk; no entry was added, because every shipped file this release edits — `README.md`, `README.template.md`, `docs/adr/0037-the-spine-and-the-resolver.md`, `docs/handbook/eras.md`, `docs/handbook/timeline.md` — was already in the manifest. Ruff: nothing to report, since no Python file is touched.",
